WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.799
I’m Adam Davis, writer/director of BROKEN
CEILING, a feature film I shot in just four

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:09.400
days in one location.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.650
The film is about a woman who after countless
disappointments and chronic mistreatment at

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.250
her job decides to turn a routine meeting
into a life-changing confrontation.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:22.740
If people would like to check it out, BROKEN
CEILING is available on iTunes, Google Play,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.060
Vudu and is free for Amazon Prime members.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.119
And I can be found at Adam Davis film on Twitter
and Instagram.

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:32.230
When I set out to write the film I had a unique
challenge.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:38.199
How do I make four people stuck in a conference
room together interesting for 90 minutes?

00:00:38.199 --> 00:00:42.610
How do I keep the tension up so that the audience
stays engaged?

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:47.510
I decided to incorporate a lot of narrative
twists and turns and use flashbacks to help

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:52.730
the audience understand how the characters
got to such a dire, dangerous situation.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:56.850
If you’re constantly keeping the audience
guessing and surprising them with unexpected

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.370
moments, they’ll be along for the whole
ride.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:05.960
I also wrote scenes taking place in the cubicle
farm and the boss’s office, in the restrooms,

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:11.160
which was able to break up the monotony of
the conference room narratively and visually.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.850
From a production standpoint, having all of
these side locations in the office building

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.800
we shot in was a godsend.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.670
The cast and crew could just land at the location
and everything we need was there.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:28.350
Spaces for hair and make-up, restrooms people
could use, a kitchen we could base lunch and

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:34.710
crafty in, there was no need to travel which
was key to shooting the film in four days.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:39.870
Since about 60% of the film takes place in
the conference room itself, I had to figure

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:44.160
out a visual style that would keep things
looking interesting as well as figuring out

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.360
the blocking to move the actors around the
room as the dynamics of the characters changed.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.220
For the visual style I had the language of
the film change as the story progressed.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.909
So I started with a lot of shots on sticks
and some steady handheld shots.

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:04.799
I did not want the camera to be noticeable
when things were just getting started.

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:10.850
As the story got more intense I switched to
a more handheld style, looser and intense

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:16.530
itself like Paul Greengrass did in the Bourne
movies or that great opening scene in JJ Abrams

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.380
MISSION IMPOSSIBLE 3.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.470
I also put the camera physically closer to
the characters so that you really felt that

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.010
you were part of the situation.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:30.920
Now for the blocking I wanted everything to
be motivated by the power dynamic in the room.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:35.840
So at the beginning of the film the boss Ken
has all the power.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.010
He’s at the head of the table lording over
the three employees.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:44.420
But as each of the employees take their shot
at the throne (so to speak) the positions

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.530
around the table change.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:51.650
Over time our protagonist Angela goes from
the assistant position way off to the side

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:58.390
unnoticeable really to slowly making her way
closer and closer to the throne.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.340
More and more in frame of the wide shots for
example.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.900
But of course the other two employees Tyler
and Garret engage in the power struggle as

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:12.120
well so their positions move and then you’re
getting this very dynamic dance motivated

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.819
by each character’s desires.

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:19.270
You couple that blocking with the visual style
and you have something that the audience can

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.320
really sink their teeth into.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:29.400
What could have been a boring, static, one-location
story becomes meaty and thematically engaging.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.900
What I would say is give yourself limitations
when you’re thinking about your next project

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.790
or your current project.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:41.410
Box yourself into a corner because you’ll
be forced to dig deep into the material to

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.070
make it as creative and interesting as possible.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:50.569
Finding the depth in the story will help keep
the cast, crew and even you really, really

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:54.090
motivated to bring it to life in new ways.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:58.780
Again, please check out the film at BROKEN
CEILING movie [dot] com and let me know your

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.750
thoughts at Adam Davis Film on Twitter and
Instagram.

